      "skos:scopeNote": "borne by: State"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010-sch-1-sec-18",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "18 Crimes Legislation Amendment (Serious and Organised Crime) Act",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010#sch-1-sec-18"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010.pdf, p.22",
      "values:originalText": "(c) the court is satisfied that particular property is subject to the\n*effective control of the person;\nthe court may make an order declaring that the whole, or a\nspecified part, of that property is available to satisfy the\nunexplained wealth order.\n\n(2) The order under subsection (1) may be enforced against the\nproperty as if the property were the *person’s property.\n\n(3) A *restraining order may be made in respect of the property as if:\n\n(a) the property were the *person’s property; and\n\n(b) there were reasonable grounds to suspect that:\n\n(i) the person had committed an offence against a law of\nthe Commonwealth, a *foreign indictable offence or a\n*State offence that has a federal aspect;\n\n(ii) the whole or any part of the person’s wealth was derived\nfrom an offence against a law of the Commonwealth, a\nforeign indictable offence or a State offence that has a\nfederal aspect.\n\n(4) If the *DPP applies for an order under subsection (1) relating to\nparticular property, the DPP must give written notice of the\napplication to:\n\n(a) the person who is subject to the *unexplained wealth order;\nand\n\n(b) any person whom the DPP has reason to believe may have an\n*interest in the property.\n\n(5) The person who is subject to the *unexplained wealth order, and\nany person who claims an *interest in the property, may appear and\nadduce evidence at the hearing of the application.",
      "skos:definition": "(c) the court is satisfied that particular property is subject to the\n*effective control of the person;\nthe court may make an order declaring that the whole, or a\nspecified part, of that property is available to satisfy the\nunexplained wealth order.\n\n(2) The order under subsection (1) may be enforced against the\nproperty as if the property were the *person’s property.\n\n(3) A *restraining order may be made in respect of the property as if:\n\n(a) the property were the *person’s property; and\n\n(b) there were reasonable grounds to suspect that:\n\n(i) the person had committed an offence against a law of\nthe Commonwealth, a *foreign indictable offence or a\n*State offence that has a federal aspect;\n\n(ii) the whole or any part of the person’s wealth was derived\nfrom an offence against a law of the Commonwealth, a\nforeign indictable offence or a State offence that has a\nfederal aspect.\n\n(4) If the *DPP applies for an order under subsection (1) relating to\nparticular property, the DPP must give written notice of the\napplication to:\n\n(a) the person who is subject to the *unexplained wealth order;\nand\n\n(b) any person whom the DPP has reason to believe may have an\n*interest in the property.\n\n(5) The person who is subject to the *unexplained wealth order, and\nany person who claims an *interest in the property, may appear and\nadduce evidence at the hearing of the application.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010-sch-1-sec-18-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010-sch-1-sec-18-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010-sch-1-sec-18-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010-sch-1-sec-18-ss-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "923c84258ffa12c16041e8ca4988ca551f14aa7a513ea0ce2c647681688723b3"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010#sch-1-sec-18",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "18 Crimes Legislation Amendment (Serious and Organised Crime) Act",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 22,
      "values:originalText": "(c) the court is satisfied that particular property is subject to the\n*effective control of the person;\nthe court may make an order declaring that the whole, or a\nspecified part, of that property is available to satisfy the\nunexplained wealth order.\n\n(2) The order under subsection (1) may be enforced against the\nproperty as if the property were the *person’s property.\n\n(3) A *restraining order may be made in respect of the property as if:\n\n(a) the property were the *person’s property; and\n\n(b) there were reasonable grounds to suspect that:\n\n(i) the person had committed an offence against a law of\nthe Commonwealth, a *foreign indictable offence or a\n*State offence that has a federal aspect;\n\n(ii) the whole or any part of the person’s wealth was derived\nfrom an offence against a law of the Commonwealth, a\nforeign indictable offence or a State offence that has a\nfederal aspect.\n\n(4) If the *DPP applies for an order under subsection (1) relating to\nparticular property, the DPP must give written notice of the\napplication to:\n\n(a) the person who is subject to the *unexplained wealth order;\nand\n\n(b) any person whom the DPP has reason to believe may have an\n*interest in the property.\n\n(5) The person who is subject to the *unexplained wealth order, and\nany person who claims an *interest in the property, may appear and\nadduce evidence at the hearing of the application."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010-sch-1-sec-18-ss-2",
