    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010-sch-3-sec-15gh",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "15GH Applications for authorities to conduct controlled operations",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010#sch-3-sec-15gh"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010.pdf, p.71",
      "values:originalText": "(1) An Australian law enforcement officer of a law enforcement\nagency may apply to an authorising officer for an authority to\nconduct a controlled operation on behalf of the law enforcement\nagency.\n\n(2) An application for an authority may be made:\n\n(a) by means of a written document signed by the applicant\n(such an application is a formal application); or\n\n(b) if the applicant has reason to believe that the delay caused by\nmaking a formal application may affect the success of the\ncontrolled operation—orally in person, or by telephone or\nany other means of communication (such an application is an\nurgent application).\n\n(3) Nothing in this Part prevents an application for an authority being\nmade in respect of a controlled operation that has been the subject\nof a previous application, but in that case the subsequent\napplication must be a formal application.\nNote:\nAn urgent authority can be varied, but not so as to extend its\nduration—see sections 15GO and 15GS.\n\n(4) An application (whether formal or urgent) must:\n\n(a) provide sufficient information to enable the authorising\nofficer to decide whether or not to grant the application; and\n\n(b) state whether or not the proposed controlled operation, or any\nother controlled operation with respect to the same criminal\nactivity, has been the subject of an earlier application\n(whether formal or urgent) for an authority or variation of an\nauthority and, if so, whether or not the authority was given or\nthe variation granted; and\n\n(c) state the proposed period of effect of the authority, which\nmust not exceed:\n\n(i) in the case of a formal application—3 months; and\n\n(ii) in the case of an urgent application—7 days.\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2012C00115\n\nSchedule 3  Amendments relating to controlled operations, assumed identities and\nwitness identity protection",
      "skos:definition": "(1) An Australian law enforcement officer of a law enforcement\nagency may apply to an authorising officer for an authority to\nconduct a controlled operation on behalf of the law enforcement\nagency.\n\n(2) An application for an authority may be made:\n\n(a) by means of a written document signed by the applicant\n(such an application is a formal application); or\n\n(b) if the applicant has reason to believe that the delay caused by\nmaking a formal application may affect the success of the\ncontrolled operation—orally in person, or by telephone or\nany other means of communication (such an application is an\nurgent application).\n\n(3) Nothing in this Part prevents an application for an authority being\nmade in respect of a controlled operation that has been the subject\nof a previous application, but in that case the subsequent\napplication must be a formal application.\nNote:\nAn urgent authority can be varied, but not so as to extend its\nduration—see sections 15GO and 15GS.\n\n(4) An application (whether formal or urgent) must:\n\n(a) provide sufficient information to enable the authorising\nofficer to decide whether or not to grant the application; and\n\n(b) state whether or not the proposed controlled operation, or any\nother controlled operation with respect to the same criminal\nactivity, has been the subject of an earlier application\n(whether formal or urgent) for an authority or variation of an\nauthority and, if so, whether or not the authority was given or\nthe variation granted; and\n\n(c) state the proposed period of effect of the authority, which\nmust not exceed:\n\n(i) in the case of a formal application—3 months; and\n\n(ii) in the case of an urgent application—7 days.\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2012C00115\n\nSchedule 3  Amendments relating to controlled operations, assumed identities and\nwitness identity protection",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010-sch-3-sec-15gh-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010-sch-3-sec-15gh-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010-sch-3-sec-15gh-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010-sch-3-sec-15gh-ss-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "97e8ddb5b4cf52cc3ca64c992718259ea65d7297b2f76c06e473ac9bd2f51580"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010#sch-3-sec-15gh",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "15GH Applications for authorities to conduct controlled operations",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 71,
      "values:originalText": "(1) An Australian law enforcement officer of a law enforcement\nagency may apply to an authorising officer for an authority to\nconduct a controlled operation on behalf of the law enforcement\nagency.\n\n(2) An application for an authority may be made:\n\n(a) by means of a written document signed by the applicant\n(such an application is a formal application); or\n\n(b) if the applicant has reason to believe that the delay caused by\nmaking a formal application may affect the success of the\ncontrolled operation—orally in person, or by telephone or\nany other means of communication (such an application is an\nurgent application).\n\n(3) Nothing in this Part prevents an application for an authority being\nmade in respect of a controlled operation that has been the subject\nof a previous application, but in that case the subsequent\napplication must be a formal application.\nNote:\nAn urgent authority can be varied, but not so as to extend its\nduration—see sections 15GO and 15GS.\n\n(4) An application (whether formal or urgent) must:\n\n(a) provide sufficient information to enable the authorising\nofficer to decide whether or not to grant the application; and\n\n(b) state whether or not the proposed controlled operation, or any\nother controlled operation with respect to the same criminal\nactivity, has been the subject of an earlier application\n(whether formal or urgent) for an authority or variation of an\nauthority and, if so, whether or not the authority was given or\nthe variation granted; and\n\n(c) state the proposed period of effect of the authority, which\nmust not exceed:\n\n(i) in the case of a formal application—3 months; and\n\n(ii) in the case of an urgent application—7 days.\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2012C00115\n\nSchedule 3  Amendments relating to controlled operations, assumed identities and\nwitness identity protection"
    },
