    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010-sch-3-sec-20",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "20 Assumed identities—continuation of old law in relation to",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010#sch-3-sec-20"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010.pdf, p.166",
      "values:originalText": "States without corresponding laws\n(1)\nDespite the repeal of Part IAC of the Crimes Act 1914 by this Schedule:\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2012C00115\n\nAmendments relating to controlled operations, assumed identities and witness identity\nprotection  Schedule 3\nGeneral application and transitional provisions  Part 3\n\nCrimes Legislation Amendment (Serious and Organised Crime) Act 2010\n\n(a) section 15XH of that Act, as in force immediately before the\ncommencement of this item, continues in effect after that\ncommencement, in relation to a State or Territory that is not a\nparticipating jurisdiction, as if that repeal had not happened;\nand\n\n(b) that Part, and any other provision of the Crimes Act 1914 that\nrelates to the operation of that Part, continues in effect in\nrelation to authorisations under that Part and assumed\nidentities acquired or used under such authorisations as if that\nrepeal had not happened.\n(2)\nIn this item:\nparticipating jurisdiction has the same meaning as in section 15K of\nthe Crimes Act 1914, as in force immediately after the commencement\nof this item.\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2012C00115\n\nSchedule 3  Amendments relating to controlled operations, assumed identities and\nwitness identity protection",
      "skos:definition": "States without corresponding laws\n(1)\nDespite the repeal of Part IAC of the Crimes Act 1914 by this Schedule:\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2012C00115\n\nAmendments relating to controlled operations, assumed identities and witness identity\nprotection  Schedule 3\nGeneral application and transitional provisions  Part 3\n\nCrimes Legislation Amendment (Serious and Organised Crime) Act 2010\n\n(a) section 15XH of that Act, as in force immediately before the\ncommencement of this item, continues in effect after that\ncommencement, in relation to a State or Territory that is not a\nparticipating jurisdiction, as if that repeal had not happened;\nand\n\n(b) that Part, and any other provision of the Crimes Act 1914 that\nrelates to the operation of that Part, continues in effect in\nrelation to authorisations under that Part and assumed\nidentities acquired or used under such authorisations as if that\nrepeal had not happened.\n(2)\nIn this item:\nparticipating jurisdiction has the same meaning as in section 15K of\nthe Crimes Act 1914, as in force immediately after the commencement\nof this item.\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2012C00115\n\nSchedule 3  Amendments relating to controlled operations, assumed identities and\nwitness identity protection",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010-sch-3-sec-20-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010-sch-3-sec-20-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "c93952136c0bd313fe4fe8043cb109dda7b43687bb00db6e50d07806682d62a7",
      "skos:note": "States without corresponding laws: continuation of old law in relation to"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-legislation-amendment-serious-and-organised-crime-act-2010#sch-3-sec-20",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "20 Assumed identities—continuation of old law in relation to",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 166,
      "values:originalText": "States without corresponding laws\n(1)\nDespite the repeal of Part IAC of the Crimes Act 1914 by this Schedule:\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2012C00115\n\nAmendments relating to controlled operations, assumed identities and witness identity\nprotection  Schedule 3\nGeneral application and transitional provisions  Part 3\n\nCrimes Legislation Amendment (Serious and Organised Crime) Act 2010\n\n(a) section 15XH of that Act, as in force immediately before the\ncommencement of this item, continues in effect after that\ncommencement, in relation to a State or Territory that is not a\nparticipating jurisdiction, as if that repeal had not happened;\nand\n\n(b) that Part, and any other provision of the Crimes Act 1914 that\nrelates to the operation of that Part, continues in effect in\nrelation to authorisations under that Part and assumed\nidentities acquired or used under such authorisations as if that\nrepeal had not happened.\n(2)\nIn this item:\nparticipating jurisdiction has the same meaning as in section 15K of\nthe Crimes Act 1914, as in force immediately after the commencement\nof this item.\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2012C00115\n\nSchedule 3  Amendments relating to controlled operations, assumed identities and\nwitness identity protection"
    },
